[Event "Capablanca endings: Spend a reserve pawn move"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/4k3/8/4K3/8/4P3/8_w_-_-_2_2"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/3rgJ8AAv"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/8/4k3/8/4K3/8/4P3/8 w - - 2 2"]
[ChapterId "E01"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "King and tempo"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 6, critical-position extract"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Capablanca endings. Use a reserve pawn move: push your pawn to e3. [%cal Ye2e3] } 2. e3 { Your pawn move gives Black the turn. Your king can then step past his. [%cal Ge2e3] } (2. Kf4 { Your king can go to f4 instead. It still keeps the result. } 2... Kf6 { Also winning. Your king can take this route and still keep the pawn move in reserve for later. [%cal Be6f6] }) (2. Kd4 { The square d4 works just as well for your king. The result does not change. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. This way works too, and the pawn move is still there when you need it. [%cal Be6d6] }) (2. Kf3 { Your king can also try f3, but that changes the result. } 2... Kf5 3. Ke3 3... Ke5 4. Kf3 4... Kf5 { The result has changed: shuffling the king alone lets him mirror you forever, and the win slips away without the pawn move in hand. [%cal Re5f5] }) (2. Ke3 { The e3 square looks natural for your king, but it changes the result. } 2... Ke5 3. Kf3 3... Kf5 4. Ke3 4... Ke5 { The result has changed: he mirrors every king move, and without spending the pawn tempo you can never force him aside. [%cal Rf5e5] }) 2... Kf6 { His king moves aside. Bring your king to d5. [%cal Ye4d5] } 3. Kd5 { Your king slips past his and takes d5. The pawn can wait on e3: the king travels first. [%cal Ge4d5] } (3. Kd4 { Heading to d4 is fine too. Same result, different road. } 3... Ke6 { Also winning. Your king can go here as well; the reserve pawn move is still waiting. [%cal Bf6e6] }) (3. Kf4 { Your king could step to f4 instead, and the result holds. } 3... Kg7 { Also winning. This square also wins, and you still hold the spare pawn move. [%cal Bf6g7] }) (3. Kd3 { Stepping to d3 changes the result instead of keeping it. } 3... Ke5 4. Kd2 4... Kf6 5. Ke2 5... Ke6 { The result has changed: retreating gives back the ground your king had gained. [%cal Rf6e6] }) (3. Kf3 { Your king heading to f3 throws the result away. } 3... Ke7 4. Ke4 4... Ke6 5. Kf4 5... Kf6 { The result has changed: wandering like this lets his king reclaim the opposition. [%cal Re6f6] }) 3... Ke7 { He hurries back to block the road. Stand right in front of him: king to e5. [%cal Yd5e5] } 4. Ke5 { The kings look straight at each other with one square between them, and it is his turn to move. He has to step aside. [%csl Ge5,Re7] } (4. e4 { The pawn charges off alone. Your king was supposed to go first. } 4... Kd7 5. e5 5... Ke7 6. e6 { His king parks in front of the pawn on e7 and your king can never shift it. The win is gone. [%csl Re7] }) (4. Kc5 { Your king wanders to the side and hands him the road back. } 4... Ke6 5. e4 5... Ke5 6. Kb4 { His king stands square in front of your pawn on e5 and holds it there for ever. Only a draw. [%csl Re5] }) (4. Kd4 { Backwards, and the ground your king fought for goes straight back to him. } 4... Kd6 5. e4 5... Ke6 6. e5 { He holds the square in front of your pawn and your king never gets ahead of it again. Drawn. [%csl Re6] }) (4. Ke4 { Your king steps back in front of its own pawn and stops it dead. } 4... Ke6 5. Kd3 5... Kd5 6. e4+ { Your pawn checks, he steps away and strolls back, and your king has gained nothing at all. Drawn. [%csl Rd5] }) 4... Kd7 { He gives way to d7. Now go around him the other way: king to f6. [%cal Ye5f6] } 5. Kf6 { Around the other side, and your king is past his once more. Now the pawn can walk, with your king in front of it the whole way. [%cal Ge5f6][%tail] } (5. e4 { The pawn goes first again, and that is just what he is hoping for. } 5... Ke7 6. Kd4 6... Kd6 7. e5+ { Your pawn checks from in front of your own king, and his king settles down in its path. Drawn. [%csl Re5] }) (5. Kd5 { Also winning, and the kings face off once more. But stepping around to f6 is quicker. } 5... Ke7 6. Ke5 6... Kd7 7. Kf6 { You reach f6 in the end anyway, two moves later than you had to. [%cal Ge5f6] }) (5. Kf5 { This wins too. Your king is still going around him, only by a longer road. } 5... Ke7 { He faces you again, and you must spend another move crossing to f6. [%cal Bf5f6] }) (5. Kf4 { Winning as well, but your king drops a row for no reason at all. } 5... Ke6 6. Ke4 6... Kd6 7. Kf5 { Same plan, same squares, one lap of the board wasted getting there. [%cal Bf4f5] }) 5... Kc6 6. e4 6... Kd7 7. e5 7... Ke8 8. Ke6 { King first. The pawn to e6 would have let his king in front of it. } 8... Kd8 9. Kf7 9... Kc7 10. e6 10... Kb6 11. e7 11... Kc5 12. e8=Q { A new queen on e8. One spare pawn move at the start, and your king led the pawn home. [%csl Ge8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Walk around the pawn wall"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5k2/6p1/4K1P1/5P2/8/8/8/8_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/pYjRyno6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "5k2/6p1/4K1P1/5P2/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E02"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "King and tempo"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 7, critical-position extract"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ The pawn push looks tempting. Can your king prepare a better route first? } 1. Kd7 { Your king takes a detour. It can enter e7 without losing the g6 pawn. [%cal Ge6d7] } (1. Kd6 { This route through d6 keeps the result exactly the same. } 1... Ke8 { Also winning. Your king still finds a way around the pawn wall. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (1. Kd5 { Another good square, d5. The result stays with you. } 1... Ke7 { This wins too. Your king reaches the far side of the wall by another path. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (1. Ke5 { Your king can take the e5 path instead. Nothing changes. } 1... Ke7 { Still winning: your king goes around the wall from here as well. [%cal Bf8e7] }) (1. f6 { The pawn advances before your king has reached the useful squares. } 1... Kg8 2. f7+ 2... Kf8 3. Ke5 3... Ke7 { The result has changed: his king slips into e7 first and blocks the way your king needed around the wall. [%cal Rf8e7] }) 1... Kg8 { His king steps back. Where can yours take the next important square? } 2. Ke7 { Your king reaches e7. Now the f pawn can help open a route to promotion. [%cal Gd7e7] } (2. Ke8 { The e8 square is equally good for your king. The result stands. } 2... Kh8 { Also winning. Your king slips around the pawns just as well this way. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Kd6 { Going to d6 costs you nothing. The result is unchanged. } 2... Kf8 { This route wins too, and your king still gets around the wall. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kd8 { Your king has another good try in d8. Same result either way. } 2... Kh8 { Also winning. Your king finds another door around the pawn wall. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Ke6 { Your king can go to e6 instead. It still keeps the result. } 2... Kf8 { Still good: your king still circles the wall in time. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kc6 { The square c6 works just as well for your king. The result does not change. } 2... Kh8 { This also wins. Your king reaches the far side of the wall regardless. [%cal Bg8h8] }) (2. Kc7 { Heading to c7 is fine too. Same result, different road. } 2... Kf8 { Also winning. Another path around the wall works for your king. [%cal Bg8f8] }) (2. Kc8 { Your king could step to c8 instead, and the result holds. } 2... Kh8 { This wins as well. Your king still turns the corner past the pawns. [%cal Bg8h8] }) 2... Kh8 { His king is jammed into the corner on h8. Now break that wall open. } 3. f6 { Your pawn jabs into f6, right where his g pawn can take it. That is the idea: the wall comes apart. [%cal Gf5f6] } (3. Kf7 { Careful! Your king takes the last square from his king: the one on h8 cannot move and the g pawn is blocked. Stalemate, and the win is gone. [%csl Rh8] }) (3. Kf8 { Squeezing in to f8 leaves his king on h8 with nowhere at all to go, and the blocked g pawn cannot move either. Stalemate. [%csl Rh8] }) (3. Ke6 { Your king drops back to e6, and the win is still there. } 3... Kg8 { Also winning, but the wall is untouched and your king has to come back. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd6 { The d6 square keeps everything: the same win, one move later. } 3... Kg8 { His king returns to g8 and the pawn wall still stands, waiting for the break. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd7 { A quiet step to d7. The win survives, though nothing has been asked of him. } 3... Kg8 { He walks back out to g8, and the f pawn break is still waiting to be played. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Kd8 { Your king strolls to d8. Still winning, still not touching the wall. } 3... Kg8 { Out he comes to g8 again, and you have spent a move for nothing. [%cal Bh8g8] }) (3. Ke8 { The e8 square wins too, but it asks his king no questions at all. } 3... Kg8 { He steps back to g8, and the pawns on f5 and g6 are exactly where they were. [%cal Bh8g8] }) 3... gxf6 { His g pawn takes on f6. There is a free pawn there for your king, and one very tempting push. Choose carefully. } 4. Kxf6 { Your king scoops up f6. His king on h8 is alone now, and your g pawn has an escort. [%cal Ge7f6] } (4. g7+ { A check, and the pawn is one square from a queen. Look again before you play it. } 4... Kxg7 { His king just takes on g7. Your last pawn is gone, his f pawn is still marching, and now you are the one losing. [%cal Rh8g7] }) (4. Kf7 { Also winning, and even quicker than taking: your king guards g8. [%cal Gf7g8] } 4... f5 { Still winning. His pawn runs, but there is nothing for it to catch. [%cal Bf6f5] }) (4. Kf8 { The f8 square wins as well, one move slower than collecting the pawn. } 4... f5 { This wins too. His pawn is running with nobody at all to help it. [%cal Bf6f5] }) 4... Kg8 { Out he comes to g8 to stand in the pawn's way. Pawn or king: which of yours goes now? } 5. g7 { The pawn steps up to g7, and your king on f6 stands guard over it. His king cannot touch it there. [%cal Gg6g7] } (5. Ke6 { Your king wanders off to e6 and stops watching the pawn on g6. } 5... Kg7 { His king lands on g7, where it attacks the pawn on g6 and wins it. Only a draw now. [%cal Rg8g7] }) (5. Ke7 { The e7 square leaves the pawn on g6 with nobody looking after it. } 5... Kg7 { Straight to g7 he goes, and the pawn on g6 is his. The win is thrown away. [%cal Rg8g7] }) (5. Kf5 { Dropping back to f5 takes your king off guard duty one move too soon. } 5... Kg7 { His king reaches g7 and the pawn on g6 falls. That leaves a draw. [%cal Rg8g7] }) 5... Kh7 { His king has only h7. There is a queen one square away, but look where his king is standing. } 6. Kf7 { Your king takes f7 and covers g8. Now the pawn can turn into a queen with nobody able to touch her. [%tail] [%cal Gf6f7,Gf7g8] } (6. g8=Q+ { A queen, with check! And she lasts exactly one move. } 6... Kxg8 { His king on h7 was standing right beside g8 the whole time. He takes her, and bare kings mean a draw. [%csl Rg8] }) (6. Ke6 { Your king steps away from g7 and the pawn is suddenly on its own. } 6... Kxg7 { His king helps itself to the pawn on g7. Nothing is left on the board, so it is a draw. [%csl Rg7] }) (6. Kf5 { Back to f5, and there is nothing at all guarding the pawn on g7 now. } 6... Kxg7 { He collects the pawn on g7 and the board is bare. The win has evaporated. [%csl Rg7] }) 6... Kh6 7. g8=Q { Queen on g8! The wall on f5 and g6 is long gone, and his king on h6 watched your king walk right around it. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Keep the king from passing"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/8/5k2/1p5p/1P2K2P/8/8/8_w_-_-_6_4"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
