{ You could rush toward the b pawn. Can you stop his king from taking your h pawn first? } 4. Kf4 { You keep the opposition. Racing past his king would let him attack your h pawn. [%tail] [%cal Ge4f4] } (4. Kd4 { Your king steps away from the h pawn. Black can get around you. } 4... Kf5 5. Kc5 5... Kg4 6. Kxb5 6... Kxh4 { The result has changed: this lets his king slip past toward your h pawn. [%cal Rg4h4] }) (4. Kd5 { You race for the b pawn, but his king can race toward your h pawn. } 4... Kf5 5. Kc6 5... Kg4 6. Kxb5 6... Kxh4 { This move gives up the block, and the result has changed. [%cal Rg4h4] }) (4. Kd3 { Your king retreats and gives up the useful opposition. } 4... Kf5 5. Kd4 5... Kg4 6. Kc5 6... Kxh4 { The result changes here, because his king gets around yours this way. [%cal Rg4h4] }) (4. Ke3 { His king can take the opposition and push yours away. } 4... Ke5 5. Kf3 5... Kf5 6. Ke3 6... Ke5 { You lose the block this way, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf5e5] }) 4... Ke6 5. Kg5 5... Ke5 6. Kxh5 { Your king takes on h5. You are a pawn up with a free h pawn, and his king on e5 cannot watch that and his own b pawn at the same time. [%cal Gg5h5] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: King before the promotion pawn"]
