{ The pawn is close to promotion. Which must lead now: your king or your pawn? } 8. Ke6 { Your king takes the key square. Pushing the pawn first would give away the win. [%cal Gf6e6] } (8. e6 { The pawn gets ahead of your king. The opposing king can stay in its way. } 8... Kf8 9. Kg6 9... Ke8 10. Kf5 10... Ke7 { The king blocks the pawn. You needed to lead with your king. [%cal Re8e7] }) (8. Kf5 { The square f5 seems fine, but the result changes here. } 8... Kf7 9. Kg4 9... Kg6 10. Kf4 10... Kf7 { The result has changed: his king reaches the key square first this way. [%cal Rg6f7] }) (8. Kg6 { This king move to g6 is a step too far. The result changes. } 8... Ke7 9. Kg5 9... Ke6 10. Kf4 10... Kd7 { This move hands him the promotion route, and the result has changed. [%cal Re6d7] }) (8. Kg5 { Try g5, and the result flips against you. } 8... Kf7 9. Kf4 9... Kg6 10. Kg4 10... Kg7 { The result changes here, because your king arrives one move too late. [%cal Rg6g7] }) 8... Kf8 { His king yields a square. Keep the promotion route under your control. } 9. Kd7 { Your king grabs d7 and owns the promotion square. Now watch the pawn stroll up behind it. [%tail] [%cal Ge6d7] } (9. Kf6 { This route through f6 keeps the result exactly the same. } 9... Ke8 { Also winning. Your king still controls the promotion route from here. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (9. Kd6 { Another good square, d6. The result stays with you. } 9... Kf7 { This wins too. Your king still reaches the key square first. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (9. Kd5 { Your king to d5 looks reasonable, but it changes the result. } 9... Ke7 10. Ke4 10... Kf7 11. Ke3 11... Ke6 { You lose control of the route this way, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf7e6] }) (9. Kf5 { Going to f5 instead costs you the result. } 9... Kf7 10. Kg4 10... Kg6 11. Kf4 11... Kf7 { The result has changed: the promotion square needed your king there first. [%cal Rg6f7] }) 9... Kf7 10. e6+ 10... Kf6 11. e7 11... Ke5 12. e8=Q+ { A new queen on e8, with check. Your king went first the whole way, and his king on e5 never got in front of the pawn. [%csl Ge8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: One square, not two"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/6p1/8/8/7p/7k/7P/6K1_b_-_-_1_8"]
[White "Opponent"]
[Black "Student"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/qtmFvp4T"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/6p1/8/8/7p/7k/7P/6K1 b - - 1 8"]
[ChapterId "E05"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Block "King and tempo"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 23, critical position with the white king on g1 so that the single step is the winner"]
[Verification "Stockfish 18 NNUE checked; see accompanying report"]
[IntendedResult "win"]

{ Your g pawn can open with one step or two, and only one of them wins. Count the race before you push. } 8... g6 { One step, and the second one stays in your pocket for the moment you need it. [%cal Gg7g6] } (8... g5 { Two squares looks like the quick way. Watch what his king does with the corner. } 9. Kh1 9... g4 10. Kg1 10... g3 11. hxg3 11... hxg3 12. Kh1 12... g2+ 13. Kg1 13... Kg3 { Stalemate! Your pawn on g2 shuts f1 and h1, your king on g3 covers the rest, and he has no move at all. The rush threw the win away. [%csl Rg1] }) (8... Kg4 { Your king goes hunting and the pawns wait. } 9. Kg2 9... h3+ 10. Kf1 10... g6 11. Ke1 11... g5 { His king walked out to e1 while you shuffled, and from there it stops everything. Drawn. [%csl Re1] }) 9. Kf1 { His king sets off across the board. There is a free pawn sitting on h2. } 9... Kxh2 { You take it, and now two pawns run instead of one. [%cal Gh3h2] } (9... g5 { Also winning: you push on and leave the pawn on h2 alone. } 10. Kg1 10... g4 { Still winning, though the free pawn was there for the taking. [%cal Bh3h2] }) (9... Kg4 { Your king steps out to g4 and lets his come home. } 10. Kf2 10... h3 11. Ke1 11... g5 12. Kf1 { He is back in time and neither pawn gets through. Drawn. [%csl Rf1] }) 10. Kf2 { He hurries back toward your pawns. Start the h pawn running. [%cal Gh4h3] } 10... h3 { The h pawn takes the lead. [%cal Gh4h3] } (10... g5 { Also winning. The g pawn sets off first instead. } 11. Kf3 { Winning still: the two pawns simply change places in the queue. [%cal Bg6g5] }) (10... Kh1 { This wins too, but your king tucks into the corner for nothing. } 11. Ke1 { Also winning, and you have handed him a free move to come closer. [%cal Bf2e1] }) (10... Kh3 { Winning as well, with your king stepping back beside the pawns. } 11. Kf3 { Still winning: the same race, one square along. [%cal Bf2f3] }) 11. Kf3 { His king blocks the h pawn's road. Bring the other one up. [%cal Gg6g5] } 11... g5 { Both pawns are on the move now. [%cal Gg6g5] } (11... Kg1 { Also winning. Your king drops into the corner first. } 12. Ke3 { Winning still, though your king has walked away from its pawns. [%cal Bf3e3] }) (11... Kh1 { This wins too, from the other corner. } 12. Kg3 { Also winning, and his king is closer than you needed to let it come. [%cal Bf3g3] }) 12. Kg4 { His king attacks both pawns at once. Only one of them can be saved. } 12... Kg2 { Your king guards the h pawn and lets the g pawn go. Now watch the one that matters. [%tail] [%cal Gg2h3] } (12... Kg1 { Your king drops away and leaves the h pawn with nobody beside it. } 13. Kxh3 13... g4+ 14. Kxg4 { Both pawns are gone and only the two kings are left. Drawn. [%csl Rg4] }) (12... Kh1 { The corner is no safer: the h pawn still stands alone. } 13. Kxh3 13... g4+ 14. Kxg4 { He collects them both and the board empties out. That is a draw, not the win you had. [%csl Rg4] }) 13. Kf5 13... h2 14. Ke4 14... h1=Q { A new queen on h1. That single step at the start kept the second one in reserve, and the spare move decided the race. [%csl Gh1] } 0-1


