{ Your f pawn has advanced. Bring your king close enough to help the majority. } 2. Ke5 { Your king supports the advance. The g and h pawns can join in. [%tail] [%cal Gd4e5] } (2. g5 { Your pawn can advance to g5 instead. It still keeps the result. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. Your king still comes forward to support the majority. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. Kd3 { Your king can take the d3 path instead. Nothing changes. } 2... Kf6 { This wins too. Your king still reaches the pawns in time to help. [%cal Be7f6] }) (2. Kd5 { The d5 square is equally good for your king. The result stands. } 2... Kf6 { Still winning: your king supports the majority just as well from here. [%cal Be7f6] }) (2. Ke4 { Going to e4 costs you nothing. The result is unchanged. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. Your king still gets there in time to help the pawns through. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. f6+ { Your pawn can also push to f6, but that changes the result. } 2... Kxf6 { His king takes, keeping the pawns connected. Your extra pawn is gone. The ending draws. [%cal Re7f6] }) (2. h5 { The h5 square looks tempting for the pawn, but it changes the result. } 2... Kf6 3. Ke4 3... h6 4. Kf3 4... Kf7 { The result has changed: the h pawn ran ahead of your king, and by the time it arrives Black has organized a defense. [%cal Rf6f7] }) 2... Kf7 { He tries to stay close and defend the pawns himself. } 3. g5 3... Ke7 4. h5 4... Kd7 5. f6 5... Kc6 6. fxg7 6... Kb5 7. g8=Q { A new queen on g8! He stayed and watched as long as he could, but three pawns against a lone king were always too many. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Sacrifice to break through"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/8/ppp4k/8/PPP5/8/8/8/7K_w_-_-_0_1"]
[White "Student"]
[Black "Opponent"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/LBFxVY8V/61QL2bYU"]
[SetUp "1"]
[FEN "8/ppp4k/8/PPP5/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 1"]
[ChapterId "E09"]
[Domain "endgame"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Block "Passed pawns"]
[Source "J. R. Capablanca, Chess Fundamentals; Part I, Example 24, critical-position extract"]
