{ You are a pawn down and defending. Move your king to d5, right in front of his. [%cal Ye5d5] } 2... Kd5 { Opposition: your kings face each other with one square between them, and whoever has to move gives way. It is his move. [%cal Ge5d5][%csl Gd5,Gd3] } (2... Kd6 { Your king retreats instead of standing directly in front of his. } 3. Kd4 3... Ke7 4. Ke5 4... Kf7 5. Kd6 { The result has changed: your king steps off the key square, and he takes the opposition. [%cal Re5d6] }) (2... Ke6 { Your king to e6 looks reasonable, but it changes the result. } 3. Ke4 3... Kf6 4. Kf4 4... Ke6 5. Ke4 { This gives up the block in front of him, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf4e4] }) (2... Kf5 { Try f5, and the result flips against you. } 3. Kd4 3... Kf6 4. Kd5 4... Ke7 5. Ke5 { The result changes here, because his king gets past this way. [%cal Rd5e5] }) (2... Kf6 { The f6 square is tempting, but the result changes there. } 3. Kd4 3... Kg6 4. Kc4 4... Kf7 5. Kd5 { You lose the front square this way, and the result has changed. [%cal Rc4d5] }) 3. e4+ { The pawn checks. Return your king to e5 and keep it in front of the pawn. [%cal Yd5e5] } 3... Ke5 { You are back in front on e5, and he cannot force your king aside. The position is a draw. [%tail][%cal Gd5e5] } (3... Kc6 { Another good square, c6. The result stays with you. } 4. Ke3 { Also drawing. Your king still holds the square directly in front of his. [%cal Bd3e3] }) (3... Ke6 { Heading to e6 is fine too. Same result, different road. } 4. Ke3 { This holds too. Your king still blocks him from here. [%cal Bd3e3] }) (3... Kc5 { Your king could step to c5 instead, and the result holds. } 4. Ke3 { Still drawing: your king still stands in front of his king. [%cal Bd3e3] }) (3... Kd6 { Your king can go to d6 instead. It still keeps the result. } 4. Kd2 { Also drawing. His king still cannot get past you this way. [%cal Bd3d2] }) 4. Kc4 4... Kd6 5. Kb5 5... Ke5 { His king has wandered too far away. Head straight back to the pawn. } 6. Kc4 6... Kxe4 { His king is nowhere close, so the pawn falls for free. Bare kings: nothing is left to fight over. Drawn. } 0-1


