{ Your a pawn is passed and his king on g6 is far from the corner. Find the square for your rook that supports the advance. } 49. Ra3 { Your rook steps behind the a pawn. The pawn can advance with protection. [%tail] [%cal Gc3a3] } (49. Rg3+ { Another good square for the rook, g3. The result stays with you. } 49... Kf5 { Also winning. Your rook still backs the pawn up from behind this way. [%cal Bg6f5] }) (49. Re3 { Your rook can take the e3 path instead. Nothing changes. } 49... Ra4 { This wins too. The rook still supports the pawn's advance from behind. [%cal Bb4a4] }) (49. Rc6 { Try c6 with the rook, and the result flips against you. } 49... Kf5 50. Rc7 50... Ra4 51. Rf7+ 51... Kg6 { The result has changed: the rook leaves the pawn's path, and Black catches up. [%cal Rf5g6] }) (49. Rc8 { Your rook's path through c8 changes the result. } 49... e5 50. Kh3 50... Rd4 51. g4 51... Ra4 { This move gives up the support from behind, and the result has changed. [%cal Rd4a4] }) (49. Rc4 { This time c4 is the wrong square for the rook. The result changes. } 49... Rxc4 50. Kg3 50... Kf5 51. a6 51... Ra4 { The result changes here, because the pawn can no longer count on the rook. [%cal Rc4a4] }) 49... Rh4+ 50. Kg1 50... Rh1+ 51. Kxh1 51... Kf5 52. a6 52... Ke4 53. a7 53... Kd4 54. a8=Q { Queen! Your pawn lands on a8, and his king on d4 is still four moves from the corner he needed to reach. [%csl Ga8] } 1-0
